// A class literal (`A.class`, `int.class`, `A[].class`) is a Class object: the
// same kind of value Object.getClass() returns, built by the same runtime
// helper, equal to it, and an instance of the prelude's Class. The literal used
// to be typed Object, so `A.class.getName()` did not compile, and it used to
// evaluate to the raw tyclass handle, which killed the process when a class
// object was printed: a tyclass starts with its name, exactly where an object
// keeps its class pointer, so println dispatched on the name and jumped
// through it.
//
// The first block below is byte for byte what javac 25 prints for the same
// program. The second block is not, and every difference is Teyru's, not the
// literal's:
//   - Class.toString is getName (lib/01_core.teyru), so printing a Class object
//     prints the bare name where Java prints "class Point" (as t77_getclass).
//   - A prelude class is named by its Teyru package, so the class of a Class
//     object is "teyru.Class" where Java says "java.lang.Class".
//   - Every array value is an instance of one synthetic class, teyru.Array, so
//     that is what `A[].class` names for any element type and `String[].class
//     == int[].class` holds; Java gives each element type its own array class
//     and spells them "[I" and "[Ljava.lang.String;". Naming those here would
//     take per-element-type array classes, which the runtime and the array
//     casts do not have -- and it would make the literal a class no array is an
//     instance of, where Java keeps the two equal (the line above the block).
//     The primitive forms are exact, because a primitive has no class of its
//     own in the program and one can be named after the type.
class Point {
  public Point() {
  }
}

class Outer {
  class Inner {
    public Inner() {
    }
  }
}

class Main {
  public static void main(String[] args) {
    System.out.println(Point.class.getName())
    System.out.println(Point.class == Point.class)
    System.out.println(Point.class == new Point().getClass())
    Class c = Point.class
    System.out.println(c.getName())
    System.out.println(c == Point.class)
    Object o = Point.class
    System.out.println(o instanceof Class)
    System.out.println(((Class) o).getName())
    System.out.println(boolean.class.getName())
    System.out.println(byte.class.getName())
    System.out.println(short.class.getName())
    System.out.println(char.class.getName())
    System.out.println(int.class.getName())
    System.out.println(long.class.getName())
    System.out.println(float.class.getName())
    System.out.println(double.class.getName())
    System.out.println(void.class.getName())
    System.out.println(int.class == int.class)
    System.out.println(int.class == Integer.class)
    System.out.println(Point.class == Integer.class)
    Object arr = new String[0]
    System.out.println(arr.getClass() == String[].class)
    System.out.println(Outer.Inner.class.getName())

    System.out.println(Point.class.getClass().getName())
    System.out.println(Point.class)
    System.out.println(Point.class.toString())
    System.out.println(String[].class.getName())
    System.out.println(int[].class.getName())
    System.out.println(String[].class == int[].class)
  }
}
